==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Baby Cats''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Cats in the Cradle''' at level 30 by using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

Evolves into '''Cats in the Stroller''' at level 60 using [[Catfruit]], [[Catfruit#Behemoth Stones|Behemoth Stones]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Although Baby Cat is able to do a lot of damage with its low cost and cooldown, it can be extremely risky to use, as its lack of defensive abilities and very low range puts it in the line of fire, not to mention its knockback ability isn't very useful either, as better options exist for both Alien and Floating enemies. More often than not, its long animation and attack delay means it can't land consistent hits, making its Freeze Immunity difficult to exploit against the likes of [[Maawth]] and [[Henry]]. For these reasons, Baby Cat is of little to no use before True Form.

==='''True Form'''===
Cats in the Cradle is a massive upgrade over its previous forms with its range gain, health boost, speed bonus and Wave Attacks. The range increase and Wave are likely the most important, as they combined allow this unit to land hits easily with extra damage in close quarters while sniping threats that are not hit by the initial attack. Its higher speed allows it to reach the frontlines faster and the health boost further increases the chances of it making a successful attack. Its other primary weaknesses remain, but this unit is still much easier to use, and the True Form is well worth its high cost. It's also recommended to level them to 31, since it increases their damage above 60,000, which allows them to kill 200% [[Otta-smack-u]] in one hit, among other important interactions.

==='''Ultra Form'''===
While this Ultra Form makes few changes, it does improve Cats in the Cradle quite a lot. The most notable change is the Omni Strike. While the piercing range is insignificant, it helps to address problems brought by the unit's long foreswing that leads to it missing often. Also, they gain an extra Wave level, allowing them to snipe backliners and clear peons with greater ease. The speed increase is decently helpful, as it allows them to quickly reach the frontlines, or to invade blindspots of LD threats like [[Queen B]] and [[Croakley]]. The knockback chance increase, on the other hand, is negligible; their gargantuan '''112,000''' damage (goes up to '''224,000'''  if the Wave Attack also connects) means they'll likely stagger enemies with raw damage anyways.

Overall, this Ultra Form is a decent improvement, however, it is not a huge priority, as Cats in the Cradle was already amazing on its own and doesn't gain any stat boosts in this form. If you own units like [[Windy (Uber Rare Cat)|Windy]], [[Mighty Kat-A-Pult (Uber Rare Cat)|Mighty Kat-A-Pult]] or [[Dioramos (Uber Rare Cat)|Dioramos]], getting their Ultra Forms before Cats in the Stroller is highly recommended. However, as this unit is primarily stat-based, they do benefit significantly when boosted to level 60, so this Ultra Form shouldn't be fully discounted either.

==='''Talents'''===
[[Immune to Curse|Curse Immunity]] is completely useless, as this unit is primarily used for its damage. [[Slow]] is too short to be useful, and this unit is again used for damage, so that doesn't change the previous point. Resistance to Slow has slightly more uses, but is just as situational and awkward, since most relevant threats won't give it a chance or are better dealt with using other units. HP Buff and especially Attack Buff are obviously good due to it being a stat-based unit, although they are still pretty expensive for what they do, so it must be sufficiently boosted with [[Catseye]]s for optimal results.

